00:08:34,540 --> 00:08:35,810
the response here,

151
00:08:35,810 --> 00:08:39,920
then we would have understood that as well.

152
00:08:39,920 --> 00:08:41,960
But anyway, now it worked.

153
00:08:41,960 --> 00:08:46,053
And so let's now render, this country here as well.

154
00:08:47,840 --> 00:08:52,840
So data2 and now let's wait, and there it is, great.

155
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:57,050
Now Actually, I created a special class,

156
00:08:57,050 --> 00:08:59,140
here for the neighboring country,

157
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:01,393
so that it appears a little bit smaller.

158
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:04,180
And so when it is the neighbor,

159
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,963
we want to attach some class here to the country.

160
00:09:07,950 --> 00:09:11,193
And so let's pass that class in.

161
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:14,400
So let's say className.

162
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:17,100
And by default, we set it to nothing.

163
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:20,430
So here, we will just edit to the string, like this.

164
00:09:23,930 --> 00:09:25,530
And now when it is a neighbor,

165
00:09:25,530 --> 00:09:30,530
we can just pass in, neighbor like this.

166
00:09:32,180 --> 00:09:36,640
Give it a safe, and yeah, so that looks a bit nicer.

167
00:09:36,640 --> 00:09:39,623
We get information that this is a neighbor country.

168
00:09:40,750 --> 00:09:45,750
And so now let's try this, for example, for the USA as well.

169
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:48,853
But now I just want to do one at a time.

170
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:52,723
So here we should probably get Mexico or Canada.

171
00:09:53,650 --> 00:09:55,190
And indeed, here it is.

172
00:09:55,190 --> 00:09:59,010
Here is Canada, the neighboring country of the US.

173
00:09:59,010 --> 00:10:00,170
And so one more time.

174
00:10:00,170 --> 00:10:03,110
This second AJAX call would not have been possible

175
00:10:03,110 --> 00:10:04,570
without the first one.

176
00:10:04,570 --> 00:10:06,610
Because otherwise, how would we know

177
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:09,973
that the second country here needed to be Canada?

178
00:10:12,130 --> 00:10:15,450
So, again, we have one AJAX call here

179
00:10:15,450 --> 00:10:17,870
that depends on another one.

180
00:10:17,870 --> 00:10:20,630
And so what we have here is one callback function

181
00:10:20,630 --> 00:10:22,303
inside of another one.

182
00:10:23,380 --> 00:10:26,640
So you see here, we attach the first callback function.

183
00:10:26,640 --> 00:10:30,690
And then inside of that, we have yet another one.

184
00:10:30,690 --> 00:10:34,720
So in other words, here, we have nested callbacks.

185
00:10:34,720 --> 00:10:36,500
But now imagine that we wanted

186
00:10:36,500 --> 00:10:39,020
to do more requests in sequence,

187
00:10:39,020 --> 00:10:41,890
like the neighbor of the neighbor of the neighbor,

188
00:10:41,890 --> 00:10:44,040
and like 10 times over.

189
00:10:44,040 --> 00:10:45,790
So in that case, we would end up

190
00:10:45,790 --> 00:10:49,200
with callbacks inside of callbacks inside of callbacks,

191
00:10:49,200 --> 00:10:51,060
like 10 times.

192
00:10:51,060 --> 00:10:53,600
And actually, for that kind of structure.

193
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:57,040
And for that kind of behavior, we have a special name.

194
00:10:57,040 --> 00:11:00,400
And that special name is callback hell.

195
00:11:00,400 --> 00:11:02,570
So basically, callback hell

196
00:11:02,570 --> 00:11:05,460
is when we have a lot of nested callbacks

197
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:09,690
in order to execute asynchronous tasks in sequence.

198
00:11:09,690 --> 00:11:13,400
And in fact, this happens for all asynchronous tasks,

199
00:11:13,400 --> 00:11:15,520
which are handled by callbacks.

200
00:11:15,520 --> 00:11:18,060
And not just AJAX calls.

201
00:11:18,060 --> 00:11:22,373
So for example, let's say we have a set timeout function.

202
00:11:25,190 --> 00:11:29,170
And then here, we want to log something to the Console,

203
00:11:29,170 --> 00:11:33,310
like 1 second passed, but then also,

204
00:11:33,310 --> 00:11:35,463
we want to start another timeout.

205
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:38,863
Let's just set 1000 here.

206
00:11:40,010 --> 00:11:41,623
So let's copy this one here.

207
00:11:42,650 --> 00:11:45,200
And so as I just said, let's say,

208
00:11:45,200 --> 00:11:47,620
that here we wanted to start a new timer

209
00:11:47,620 --> 00:11:49,883
after the first timer has finished.

210
00:11:51,190 --> 00:11:53,423
So here, we can say 2 seconds passed.

211
00:11:54,610 --> 00:11:57,190
And we can even add another one.

212
00:11:57,190 --> 00:12:01,561
So never mind about what just happened in the Console.

213
00:12:01,561 --> 00:12:04,763
And here, why not add yet another one?

214
00:12:07,690 --> 00:12:11,083
And so now they should appear like one, two, three, four.

215
00:12:12,310 --> 00:12:15,960
And so here to of course, we have callback hell.

216
00:12:15,960 --> 00:12:19,700
And in fact, callback hell is pretty easy to identify

217
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:24,700
by this triangular shape that is formed here, you see that.

218
00:12:25,150 --> 00:12:28,453
And also the same is starting to appear here.

219
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:32,633
So if we had more callbacks in here,

220
00:12:32,633 --> 00:12:36,310
then we would start to see a lot more indentation here.

221
00:12:36,310 --> 00:12:40,400
And then this triangular shape, would also appear there.

222
00:12:40,400 --> 00:12:42,280
Now, the problem with callback hell

223
00:12:42,280 --> 00:12:45,430
is that it makes our code look very messy.

224
00:12:45,430 --> 00:12:47,000
But even more important,

225
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,380
it makes our code harder to maintain,

226
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:53,340
and very difficult to understand, and to reason about,

227
00:12:53,340 --> 00:12:55,760
and code that is hard to understand

228
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:57,670
and difficult to reason about.

229
00:12:57,670 --> 00:13:01,500
Will have more bugs, and it's just worse code.

230
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:03,410
So this is a great rule

231
00:13:03,410 --> 00:13:06,410
that you should always remember and keep in mind.

232
00:13:06,410 --> 00:13:10,690
So again, the rule is that code that's hard to understand,

233
00:13:10,690 --> 00:13:14,620
is basically bad code, because it will have more bugs,

234
00:13:14,620 --> 00:13:17,050
because the harder it is to understand code

235
00:13:17,050 --> 00:13:18,860
and to reason about the code,

236
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:22,080
the more difficult it will be to add new features

237
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:25,400
and to add more functionality to the application.

238
00:13:25,400 --> 00:13:29,280
But anyway, given all these problems with callback hell,

239
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:32,810
we of course, need a way to solve callback hell.

240
00:13:32,810 --> 00:13:36,020
And fortunately for us, since ES6,

241
00:13:36,020 --> 00:13:39,660
there is actually a way of escaping callback hell

242
00:13:39,660 --> 00:13:42,570
by using something called promises.

243
00:13:42,570 --> 00:13:44,690
And so let's now take the next step

244
00:13:44,690 --> 00:13:48,000
in our journey of asynchronous JavaScript,

245
00:13:48,000 --> 00:13:50,493
which is to learn all about promises.